00:00:05.508 --> 00:00:08.488
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.488 --> 00:00:10.388
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.388 --> 00:00:13.278
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:13.288 --> 00:00:15.078
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:15.078 --> 00:00:17.778
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.788 --> 00:00:19.808
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.808 --> 00:00:21.618
time as improving your English.

00:00:22.008 --> 00:00:25.178
I'm Alastair Budge, and this is part two

00:00:25.198 --> 00:00:28.408
of our three-part mini-series on The Dark

00:00:28.408 --> 00:00:30.948
Side of Psychology, where we'll look at

00:00:30.948 --> 00:00:33.888
three of the most famous and uncomfortable

00:00:33.888 --> 00:00:36.258
experiments in the history of the mind.

00:00:36.848 --> 00:00:38.938
In part one, we looked at the

00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:40.698
Milgram experiment and the

00:00:40.718 --> 00:00:43.108
idea of obedience to authority.

00:00:43.458 --> 00:00:45.408
In part three, we'll travel to

00:00:45.408 --> 00:00:48.408
the psychiatric hospitals of 1970s

00:00:48.438 --> 00:00:51.128
America for the Rosenhan experiment.

00:00:51.378 --> 00:00:53.768
But today, in part two, we

00:00:53.768 --> 00:00:55.678
turn to the work of an American

00:00:55.678 --> 00:00:58.188
psychologist named Harry Harlow.

00:00:58.638 --> 00:01:01.398
It's a fascinating story, but I do

00:01:01.398 --> 00:01:03.178
need to give you a little warning.

00:01:03.628 --> 00:01:06.128
This episode discusses distressing

00:01:06.168 --> 00:01:08.998
experiments involving infant monkeys,

00:01:09.218 --> 00:01:11.618
including isolation and cruelty.

00:01:11.788 --> 00:01:13.878
So please do take that into

00:01:13.878 --> 00:01:15.518
account before continuing.

00:01:16.308 --> 00:01:18.348
OK then, let's not waste a

00:01:18.358 --> 00:01:20.848
minute and get right into it.

00:01:22.398 --> 00:01:23.778
"What is love?"

00:01:24.368 --> 00:01:26.368
It's a question people have been

00:01:26.418 --> 00:01:28.968
asking since the dawn of time.

00:01:29.448 --> 00:01:32.388
Aristotle, Plato, and if you

00:01:32.398 --> 00:01:35.298
grew up in the 1990s like me, the

00:01:35.298 --> 00:01:37.954
classic Eurodance song by Haddaway.

00:01:38.548 --> 00:01:41.748
It's what, all being well, you feel for

00:01:41.748 --> 00:01:44.278
the most special people in your life.

00:01:44.648 --> 00:01:47.548
It's what a parent feels for their child.

00:01:47.728 --> 00:01:49.998
It's what a child feels for their

00:01:49.998 --> 00:01:51.978
parent, some of the time at least.

00:01:52.418 --> 00:01:54.688
But where does it come from?

00:01:54.988 --> 00:01:56.898
What actually is it?

00:01:57.608 --> 00:02:00.118
Does a new-born child "love"

00:02:00.158 --> 00:02:03.818
its mother, and if so, why?

00:02:04.918 --> 00:02:08.028
Well, if you were to ask the prominent

00:02:08.138 --> 00:02:10.618
early 20th century American psychologist

00:02:11.028 --> 00:02:13.928
John B. Watson, he would tell you

00:02:14.168 --> 00:02:17.038
that love was an automatic response

00:02:17.238 --> 00:02:19.628
to being touched, being caressed.

00:02:21.068 --> 00:02:23.758
In much the same way as an infant

00:02:23.978 --> 00:02:27.018
instinctively fears certain things or

00:02:27.208 --> 00:02:29.298
gets upset when they are made to do

00:02:29.298 --> 00:02:32.698
something they do not want to do, love is

00:02:32.698 --> 00:02:36.548
an automatic reaction to external stimuli.

00:02:36.858 --> 00:02:37.628
Nothing more.

00:02:38.678 --> 00:02:40.988
And too much touching, too

00:02:40.988 --> 00:02:43.098
much affection towards a child,

00:02:43.498 --> 00:02:45.658
well, it could be dangerous.

00:02:46.228 --> 00:02:49.018
It would make them weak, clingy,

00:02:49.468 --> 00:02:52.348
dependent on their parent, unable to

00:02:52.348 --> 00:02:55.118
face the world alone when the time came.

00:02:56.487 --> 00:02:59.017
In 1928, he published his

00:02:59.017 --> 00:03:00.847
recommendations in a book,

00:03:01.647 --> 00:03:04.787
Psychological Care of Infant and Child.

00:03:05.627 --> 00:03:08.577
It was wildly successful, selling

00:03:08.587 --> 00:03:10.777
more than a hundred thousand copies

00:03:11.007 --> 00:03:13.447
in its first few months, and becoming

00:03:13.467 --> 00:03:16.737
the go-to manual on child-rearing.

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:19.597
It was unambiguous.

00:03:20.387 --> 00:03:22.587
Do not hug your child.

00:03:22.887 --> 00:03:24.037
Do not kiss them.

00:03:24.607 --> 00:03:27.107
Do not let them sit on your lap.

00:03:28.047 --> 00:03:31.737
If you must show affection, perhaps a

00:03:31.787 --> 00:03:33.927
kiss on the forehead when they go to

00:03:33.937 --> 00:03:37.117
bed, or a handshake in the morning.

00:03:38.507 --> 00:03:41.027
This might sound extraordinary now.

00:03:41.227 --> 00:03:44.157
But Watson was not a fringe figure.

00:03:44.637 --> 00:03:48.147
He was the founder of behaviourism, the

00:03:48.177 --> 00:03:51.267
dominant school in American psychology for

00:03:51.267 --> 00:03:53.877
the first half of the twentieth century.

00:03:54.977 --> 00:03:57.737
Behaviourism held that human beings

00:03:57.947 --> 00:04:00.027
could be understood as the sum

00:04:00.057 --> 00:04:01.907
of their conditioned responses.

00:04:02.317 --> 00:04:04.937
You learned what you were rewarded for.

00:04:06.057 --> 00:04:08.527
Affection, in this framework,

00:04:08.667 --> 00:04:11.207
was unnecessary, sentimental,

00:04:11.577 --> 00:04:13.477
and possibly harmful.

00:04:14.497 --> 00:04:17.467
And this had practical consequences.

00:04:18.407 --> 00:04:21.347
American hospitals often separated

00:04:21.407 --> 00:04:23.007
newborns from their mothers

00:04:23.397 --> 00:04:25.627
immediately after birth, keeping

00:04:25.627 --> 00:04:28.367
them in central nurseries to be

00:04:28.367 --> 00:04:30.647
brought out only for feeding.

00:04:31.497 --> 00:04:34.317
Orphanages and children's hospitals

00:04:34.487 --> 00:04:37.767
were quiet, clean, and clinical.

00:04:38.507 --> 00:04:40.267
Babies were fed and

00:04:40.267 --> 00:04:42.397
changed but rarely held.

00:04:43.177 --> 00:04:46.167
Some institutions even discouraged

00:04:46.177 --> 00:04:48.397
staff from picking up babies who

00:04:48.397 --> 00:04:50.607
were crying, on the grounds that this

00:04:50.607 --> 00:04:53.427
would teach them to cry for attention.

00:04:54.647 --> 00:04:58.047
Now, the dominant theory of why babies

00:04:58.067 --> 00:04:59.897
became attached to their mothers in

00:04:59.897 --> 00:05:02.567
the first place was just as cold.

00:05:03.517 --> 00:05:06.137
It was called the "cupboard love"

00:05:06.167 --> 00:05:09.617
theory, and the logic ran like this.

00:05:10.337 --> 00:05:11.947
The baby is hungry.

00:05:12.347 --> 00:05:14.357
The mother provides food.

00:05:14.797 --> 00:05:17.147
The baby learns to associate the

00:05:17.147 --> 00:05:20.077
mother with the satisfaction of hunger.

00:05:20.877 --> 00:05:23.077
Therefore the baby comes

00:05:23.077 --> 00:05:24.177
to love the mother.

00:05:25.117 --> 00:05:27.287
Love, in other words, was

00:05:27.287 --> 00:05:29.147
a side effect of feeding.

00:05:29.287 --> 00:05:31.847
It was learned, not innate.

00:05:32.307 --> 00:05:33.937
It was about the cupboard, the

00:05:34.137 --> 00:05:37.057
source of the food, not the person.

00:05:37.727 --> 00:05:40.657
So, don't give your child too much

00:05:40.697 --> 00:05:44.157
affection, hold and feed them only

00:05:44.157 --> 00:05:46.487
when necessary, otherwise they will

00:05:46.487 --> 00:05:48.857
become emotionally dependent on you.

00:05:49.997 --> 00:05:53.507
Now, even by the late 1940s, there

00:05:53.547 --> 00:05:56.107
was increasing evidence to suggest

00:05:56.117 --> 00:05:58.717
this theory might not hold water,

00:05:59.187 --> 00:06:01.127
and that the results from treating

00:06:01.137 --> 00:06:03.497
infants in this way were mixed.

00:06:04.597 --> 00:06:07.757
A psychoanalyst named René Spitz had

00:06:07.767 --> 00:06:09.737
documented the mortality rates of

00:06:09.767 --> 00:06:12.477
infants in orphanages and foundling

00:06:12.487 --> 00:06:15.287
hospitals across Europe and the Americas.

00:06:15.777 --> 00:06:17.737
The babies were being fed.

00:06:17.947 --> 00:06:19.247
They were being kept warm.

00:06:19.997 --> 00:06:21.957
On the surface, their basic

00:06:21.967 --> 00:06:23.927
physical needs were being met;

00:06:24.187 --> 00:06:26.037
they were being treated properly.

00:06:26.977 --> 00:06:29.957
And yet they were dying, in some

00:06:29.977 --> 00:06:32.547
institutions at rates of over thirty

00:06:32.547 --> 00:06:35.117
per cent in the first year of life.

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:39.240
The survivors were often developmentally

00:06:39.250 --> 00:06:42.160
damaged, withdrawn, and unable

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:44.120
to form normal relationships.

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:47.280
Spitz documented babies

00:06:47.510 --> 00:06:49.220
who had stopped vocalising.

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:50.920
Babies who had stopped

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:52.370
reaching for objects.

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:55.290
Babies who lay in their cots with

00:06:55.340 --> 00:06:57.900
empty eyes, no longer responding

00:06:58.110 --> 00:06:59.530
when staff approached them.

00:07:00.790 --> 00:07:02.840
In the medical language of the time,

00:07:03.230 --> 00:07:05.710
they were said to have wasted away.

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:10.084
Spitz called what he saw "hospitalism."

00:07:11.050 --> 00:07:13.620
Something about the absence of physical

00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:16.160
contact and emotional connection, he

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:18.500
thought, was killing these children.

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:22.610
He couldn't prove it, and the

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:24.220
dominant theory of the time

00:07:24.320 --> 00:07:26.620
— behaviourism — suggested that

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:28.350
there was nothing wrong with this.

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:30.880
Physical contact was not just

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:33.540
unnecessary, but could also be

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:36.290
dangerous to the development of a child.

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:40.580
Yet what Spitz saw in these hospitals

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:43.230
and orphanages suggested otherwise.

00:07:44.410 --> 00:07:47.800
What the field needed was an experiment.

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:52.200
In 1957, in a laboratory at the University

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:54.690
of Wisconsin, a forty-eight-year-old

00:07:54.690 --> 00:07:57.910
psychologist named Harry Harlow began

00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:00.010
work on what would become the most

00:08:00.070 --> 00:08:02.800
influential set of experiments in the

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:05.480
history of developmental psychology.

00:08:06.766 --> 00:08:08.966
Harlow ran the Primate

00:08:08.966 --> 00:08:10.946
Laboratory at Wisconsin.

00:08:11.926 --> 00:08:14.556
He had been studying rhesus monkeys

00:08:14.566 --> 00:08:17.446
for years, mostly investigating

00:08:17.446 --> 00:08:19.836
their problem-solving and learning.

00:08:20.946 --> 00:08:23.406
He needed a steady supply of monkeys,

00:08:23.706 --> 00:08:26.468
and would raise them in his laboratory,

00:08:26.826 --> 00:08:29.846
often in isolation, for the practical

00:08:29.846 --> 00:08:33.016
purpose of keeping them disease-free.

00:08:34.176 --> 00:08:36.806
And he noticed something strange

00:08:36.946 --> 00:08:38.936
about the baby monkeys, the ones

00:08:39.146 --> 00:08:40.616
who were being raised without

00:08:40.646 --> 00:08:42.736
any contact with their mother, or

00:08:42.806 --> 00:08:44.186
any other monkey for that matter.

00:08:45.316 --> 00:08:48.686
They clung to the cloth nappies that

00:08:48.696 --> 00:08:50.636
lined the floors of their cages.

00:08:51.206 --> 00:08:52.756
If the nappies were taken

00:08:52.756 --> 00:08:54.696
away to be cleaned, the baby

00:08:54.696 --> 00:08:56.816
monkeys became distressed.

00:08:57.156 --> 00:08:58.556
They wrapped themselves around

00:08:58.556 --> 00:09:01.126
the cloth as if it were a parent.

00:09:02.016 --> 00:09:04.426
These baby monkeys seemingly

00:09:04.796 --> 00:09:06.726
just wanted to hold something.

00:09:07.066 --> 00:09:09.216
They craved contact.

00:09:10.736 --> 00:09:14.716
This made no sense when looked at through

00:09:14.716 --> 00:09:16.926
the lens of the theories of the time.

00:09:17.666 --> 00:09:20.586
The cloth nappies served no physical

00:09:20.616 --> 00:09:22.646
purpose — they didn't provide food.

00:09:23.066 --> 00:09:26.106
So why were the monkeys forming

00:09:26.106 --> 00:09:27.596
what looked like an emotional

00:09:27.596 --> 00:09:29.896
attachment to a piece of fabric?

00:09:31.321 --> 00:09:32.911
Harlow designed an

00:09:32.911 --> 00:09:34.741
experiment to investigate.

00:09:35.771 --> 00:09:38.271
He built two surrogate mothers.

00:09:38.891 --> 00:09:42.021
Each was roughly the size of a rhesus

00:09:42.021 --> 00:09:44.691
monkey, with a crude face stuck

00:09:44.701 --> 00:09:46.661
on the top to look like a head.

00:09:47.781 --> 00:09:51.181
One was made of bare wire mesh.

00:09:51.571 --> 00:09:54.361
It was hard, cold, and

00:09:54.421 --> 00:09:55.861
uncomfortable to touch.

00:09:56.441 --> 00:09:58.941
But it had a bottle of milk,

00:09:59.151 --> 00:10:00.951
fitted into its chest, from

00:10:00.951 --> 00:10:02.881
which the baby monkey could feed.

00:10:04.481 --> 00:10:06.151
The other surrogate was

00:10:06.151 --> 00:10:08.461
wrapped in soft, brown cloth.

00:10:08.951 --> 00:10:10.271
It was warm.

00:10:10.281 --> 00:10:11.471
It was comfortable.

00:10:11.961 --> 00:10:14.031
But it had no milk.

00:10:15.351 --> 00:10:17.421
Both surrogates looked

00:10:17.491 --> 00:10:18.811
more or less the same.

00:10:19.311 --> 00:10:22.431
And if the cupboard love theory was

00:10:22.431 --> 00:10:25.651
correct, the baby monkeys should bond

00:10:25.821 --> 00:10:28.281
with the milk-providing wire mother.

00:10:28.681 --> 00:10:31.141
That was where their food came from.

00:10:31.531 --> 00:10:34.231
That was where, according to behaviourist

00:10:34.241 --> 00:10:37.151
theory, attachment should form.

00:10:38.931 --> 00:10:40.451
As you might have guessed,

00:10:40.891 --> 00:10:42.691
that is not what happened.

00:10:43.491 --> 00:10:46.331
The baby monkeys spent almost all their

00:10:46.331 --> 00:10:49.451
time clinging to the cloth mother.

00:10:50.161 --> 00:10:51.831
They went to the wire mother

00:10:52.301 --> 00:10:53.671
only when they were hungry.

00:10:54.161 --> 00:10:57.001
They fed quickly, and returned.

00:10:57.491 --> 00:10:59.531
They slept on the cloth mother.

00:11:00.251 --> 00:11:02.111
When something frightening was

00:11:02.111 --> 00:11:04.601
introduced into the cage, like a loud

00:11:04.631 --> 00:11:06.511
mechanical toy banging across the

00:11:06.511 --> 00:11:10.081
floor, they ran to the cloth mother and

00:11:10.081 --> 00:11:13.171
clung to her for comfort, even though

00:11:13.461 --> 00:11:15.051
she had never given them anything.

00:11:16.531 --> 00:11:18.501
The baby monkeys were bonding

00:11:18.801 --> 00:11:20.561
to the source of comfort,

00:11:20.981 --> 00:11:23.471
not to the source of food.

00:11:24.431 --> 00:11:27.301
Contact, warmth, softness.

00:11:27.421 --> 00:11:30.271
It seemed that these were as much of

00:11:30.271 --> 00:11:33.151
a basic need as the need for food.

00:11:34.771 --> 00:11:38.401
In 1958, Harlow gave a now-famous

00:11:38.411 --> 00:11:40.701
address to the American Psychological

00:11:40.701 --> 00:11:44.361
Association titled "The Nature of Love."

00:11:45.711 --> 00:11:47.551
He stood in front of his colleagues,

00:11:47.811 --> 00:11:50.011
many of whom had spent their careers

00:11:50.011 --> 00:11:53.151
explaining love as a secondary, learned

00:11:53.151 --> 00:11:56.541
response, and he told them in no

00:11:56.611 --> 00:11:59.411
uncertain terms that they were wrong.

00:12:00.371 --> 00:12:03.981
Love, he said, was about touch.

00:12:04.031 --> 00:12:05.841
It was about closeness.

00:12:05.931 --> 00:12:08.591
It was a primary biological

00:12:08.591 --> 00:12:10.411
need, not a luxury.

00:12:11.941 --> 00:12:13.921
This was followed up with

00:12:13.921 --> 00:12:15.771
a series of different and

00:12:16.021 --> 00:12:18.131
ever-more-controversial experiments.

00:12:19.251 --> 00:12:22.091
In one famous extension, called the

00:12:22.161 --> 00:12:25.581
"open field" test, Harlow placed

00:12:25.591 --> 00:12:28.851
baby monkeys in a strange, unfamiliar

00:12:28.851 --> 00:12:31.611
room full of objects, sometimes with

00:12:31.611 --> 00:12:33.991
their cloth mother, sometimes with

00:12:33.991 --> 00:12:36.541
their wire mother, sometimes alone.

00:12:37.521 --> 00:12:40.301
The babies with the cloth mother, after

00:12:40.321 --> 00:12:43.211
an initial moment of fear, would run to

00:12:43.211 --> 00:12:46.081
her, cling to her and calm themselves.

00:12:46.901 --> 00:12:48.851
And then, slowly, they

00:12:48.851 --> 00:12:50.071
would begin to explore.

00:12:50.721 --> 00:12:52.461
They would venture out from

00:12:52.461 --> 00:12:54.751
the cloth mother to investigate

00:12:54.751 --> 00:12:56.945
a toy, then run back to her.

00:12:57.311 --> 00:12:58.831
Then venture further.

00:12:59.541 --> 00:13:02.011
The cloth mother was a base,

00:13:02.041 --> 00:13:04.001
a place of safety from which

00:13:04.001 --> 00:13:05.601
the world could be approached.

00:13:06.541 --> 00:13:08.451
The babies with the wire

00:13:08.451 --> 00:13:10.731
mother showed none of this.

00:13:11.251 --> 00:13:14.551
They sat in a corner, paralysed by fear.

00:13:15.331 --> 00:13:17.241
They did not approach the wire mother for

00:13:17.241 --> 00:13:19.891
comfort, because she did not provide any.

00:13:20.601 --> 00:13:21.711
They did not explore.

00:13:22.221 --> 00:13:23.971
The world remained, for

00:13:23.971 --> 00:13:26.531
them, an unmanageable place.

00:13:28.011 --> 00:13:29.911
This was the discovery that

00:13:29.911 --> 00:13:32.411
gave attachment its place in

00:13:32.411 --> 00:13:34.171
modern developmental psychology.

00:13:34.991 --> 00:13:37.101
A loving, comforting parent

00:13:37.391 --> 00:13:39.531
was not just nice to have.

00:13:39.831 --> 00:13:41.661
The parent was, literally, the

00:13:41.671 --> 00:13:43.871
foundation from which children built

00:13:43.881 --> 00:13:46.471
the courage to engage with the world.

00:13:47.642 --> 00:13:50.777
Harlow also showed that monkeys raised

00:13:50.807 --> 00:13:53.527
entirely without any mother at all, even

00:13:53.527 --> 00:13:57.217
a fake one, grew up profoundly damaged.

00:13:57.977 --> 00:13:59.167
They could not socialise

00:13:59.187 --> 00:14:00.037
with other monkeys.

00:14:00.307 --> 00:14:01.267
They could not mate.

00:14:01.787 --> 00:14:04.107
The females who did become mothers,

00:14:04.197 --> 00:14:06.617
through artificial insemination, were

00:14:06.627 --> 00:14:09.147
often unable to care for their own babies.

00:14:09.627 --> 00:14:11.937
Some attacked them, while others

00:14:11.967 --> 00:14:14.057
ignored them as they starved.

00:14:14.567 --> 00:14:16.477
It seemed like if they had never

00:14:16.477 --> 00:14:18.547
felt maternal comfort in their

00:14:18.547 --> 00:14:21.267
infancy they were utterly unable

00:14:21.267 --> 00:14:23.047
to provide it to their offspring.

00:14:24.267 --> 00:14:26.667
Now, the implication for human

00:14:26.667 --> 00:14:29.007
beings was hard to ignore.

00:14:29.867 --> 00:14:32.967
If a baby monkey needed soft, warm

00:14:33.007 --> 00:14:35.787
contact in order to develop normally,

00:14:36.227 --> 00:14:38.857
what did that say about us, about

00:14:39.097 --> 00:14:41.577
the babies in those silent, clinical

00:14:41.617 --> 00:14:44.137
orphanage wards, about the prevailing

00:14:44.137 --> 00:14:47.157
advice to mothers not to hug or show

00:14:47.197 --> 00:14:48.757
affection towards their children?

00:14:50.527 --> 00:14:53.717
By the early 1960s, Harlow had

00:14:53.717 --> 00:14:55.787
become one of the most influential

00:14:55.967 --> 00:14:57.587
psychologists in America.

00:14:58.397 --> 00:14:59.207
He had been on the

00:14:59.217 --> 00:15:01.347
cover of news magazines.

00:15:01.747 --> 00:15:03.397
His monkey experiments were

00:15:03.397 --> 00:15:06.007
used as examples in every

00:15:06.087 --> 00:15:08.077
introductory psychology textbook.

00:15:08.937 --> 00:15:11.217
But the work he is now most

00:15:11.267 --> 00:15:13.927
criticised for was still to come.

00:15:15.207 --> 00:15:19.377
In the 1960s and into the 1970s, Harlow's

00:15:19.407 --> 00:15:22.337
work on isolation became more extreme,

00:15:22.757 --> 00:15:25.257
and I'm sorry but here's where it starts

00:15:25.257 --> 00:15:27.257
to become a little more difficult.

00:15:28.347 --> 00:15:30.737
He wanted to study not just the

00:15:30.797 --> 00:15:34.317
development of love, but its absence.

00:15:34.987 --> 00:15:36.907
What happens to a creature

00:15:37.177 --> 00:15:39.657
deprived of comfort entirely?

00:15:41.397 --> 00:15:43.847
He built what he called a "vertical

00:15:43.917 --> 00:15:47.057
chamber apparatus." It was a small,

00:15:47.297 --> 00:15:50.147
stainless steel box, shaped like an

00:15:50.197 --> 00:15:53.647
upside-down pyramid, with smooth, sloping

00:15:53.647 --> 00:15:56.457
sides that prevented any kind of grip.

00:15:57.717 --> 00:16:00.457
Baby monkeys were placed inside,

00:16:00.817 --> 00:16:03.467
alone, for periods ranging from

00:16:03.467 --> 00:16:05.787
a few days to months at a time.

00:16:07.027 --> 00:16:09.027
In his notes, Harlow called

00:16:09.027 --> 00:16:11.277
it "the pit of despair."

00:16:12.557 --> 00:16:15.637
The monkeys that came out were broken.

00:16:16.237 --> 00:16:18.717
They sat in corners, rocking back

00:16:18.717 --> 00:16:20.637
and forth, hugging themselves.

00:16:21.297 --> 00:16:22.587
They would not eat properly.

00:16:22.847 --> 00:16:24.027
They would not interact

00:16:24.197 --> 00:16:25.187
with other monkeys.

00:16:25.697 --> 00:16:27.987
They were the closest thing anyone had

00:16:28.057 --> 00:16:31.037
ever produced to a clinical model of

00:16:31.047 --> 00:16:33.737
severe depression in a non-human animal.

00:16:35.467 --> 00:16:37.087
Harlow's official purpose

00:16:37.237 --> 00:16:39.057
was research into depression.

00:16:40.167 --> 00:16:41.867
He believed that if he could

00:16:41.877 --> 00:16:43.777
produce something like depression

00:16:44.097 --> 00:16:46.507
in an animal, he could study its

00:16:46.527 --> 00:16:49.477
mechanisms and perhaps find treatments

00:16:49.667 --> 00:16:51.107
that would help human beings.

00:16:51.977 --> 00:16:54.807
And his work did produce real findings

00:16:54.807 --> 00:16:57.337
about the biology of depression and the

00:16:57.337 --> 00:16:59.937
long-term effects of early deprivation.

00:17:00.627 --> 00:17:04.697
But the cost was extreme, and the

00:17:04.697 --> 00:17:07.407
design of the experiments was, even by

00:17:07.407 --> 00:17:09.957
the standards of the time, shocking.

00:17:10.917 --> 00:17:13.337
Today, experiments like these would

00:17:13.347 --> 00:17:15.817
be considered ethically indefensible.

00:17:16.747 --> 00:17:19.517
And even back then, many of his

00:17:19.627 --> 00:17:21.877
own colleagues were uncomfortable.

00:17:22.207 --> 00:17:23.387
His graduate students

00:17:23.647 --> 00:17:25.767
sometimes refused to take part.

00:17:26.537 --> 00:17:29.047
Yet he went further still.

00:17:29.937 --> 00:17:32.807
In another set of experiments, he created

00:17:32.967 --> 00:17:35.357
what he called "rejecting mothers."

00:17:36.667 --> 00:17:38.597
These were surrogate mothers

00:17:38.897 --> 00:17:41.247
built to actively harm the

00:17:41.247 --> 00:17:43.157
baby monkey clinging to them.

00:17:44.337 --> 00:17:47.937
One model would suddenly blow out cold

00:17:47.967 --> 00:17:50.437
compressed air, blasting the baby off.

00:17:51.277 --> 00:17:53.787
Another would shake violently.

00:17:54.507 --> 00:17:56.647
Another would extend brass

00:17:56.717 --> 00:17:58.547
spikes from its surface.

00:17:59.647 --> 00:18:02.727
In each case, the baby monkey, after being

00:18:02.727 --> 00:18:06.317
attacked, would crawl back and cling to

00:18:06.317 --> 00:18:08.707
the rejecting surrogate even more tightly.

00:18:09.407 --> 00:18:11.277
There was nowhere else to go.

00:18:12.747 --> 00:18:15.057
At this point, even some people

00:18:15.057 --> 00:18:17.027
who accepted the scientific

00:18:17.037 --> 00:18:18.737
importance of his earlier work

00:18:19.017 --> 00:18:20.957
thought he had crossed a line.

00:18:22.037 --> 00:18:24.267
Now, Harlow was not the only

00:18:24.267 --> 00:18:26.427
professional working on this idea.

00:18:27.077 --> 00:18:29.097
Across the Atlantic, a British

00:18:29.137 --> 00:18:31.697
psychiatrist named John Bowlby

00:18:31.917 --> 00:18:33.727
was developing what would become

00:18:33.727 --> 00:18:35.687
known as attachment theory.

00:18:36.467 --> 00:18:39.287
This was the idea that the bond between

00:18:39.287 --> 00:18:41.947
a baby and its primary caregiver

00:18:42.237 --> 00:18:44.697
is a fundamental psychological

00:18:44.697 --> 00:18:47.457
need with lifelong consequences.

00:18:48.827 --> 00:18:50.327
Bowlby's interest in the subject

00:18:50.367 --> 00:18:52.737
was, in some ways, personal.

00:18:53.987 --> 00:18:56.127
He had grown up in an upper-class

00:18:56.177 --> 00:18:58.427
English family in which the parents

00:18:58.617 --> 00:19:00.827
barely interacted with their children.

00:19:02.087 --> 00:19:05.567
He was raised primarily by a beloved

00:19:05.617 --> 00:19:08.677
nanny named Minnie, who sounded

00:19:08.677 --> 00:19:10.867
like a sort of Mary Poppins figure.

00:19:11.947 --> 00:19:15.027
When Bowlby was four years old, his

00:19:15.057 --> 00:19:18.567
parents fired Minnie, on the grounds that

00:19:18.577 --> 00:19:21.239
the boy was becoming too attached to her.

00:19:22.367 --> 00:19:25.957
The loss devastated him, and he wrote

00:19:26.157 --> 00:19:28.317
that it had been an experience as

00:19:28.357 --> 00:19:30.807
painful as the death of a parent.

00:19:32.007 --> 00:19:34.407
His work, the work that came to

00:19:34.407 --> 00:19:36.697
define the field of attachment, was

00:19:36.697 --> 00:19:38.977
in part an attempt to understand

00:19:39.157 --> 00:19:41.897
what had been done to him as a child.

00:19:43.417 --> 00:19:46.167
Bowlby and Harlow were in frequent

00:19:46.187 --> 00:19:48.407
correspondence, and Harlow's

00:19:48.457 --> 00:19:50.477
monkey experiments supported

00:19:50.497 --> 00:19:52.497
Bowlby's theoretical framework.

00:19:53.437 --> 00:19:55.737
Together, their work transformed

00:19:55.747 --> 00:19:58.287
the field, and had enormous

00:19:58.347 --> 00:19:59.707
practical consequences.

00:20:00.407 --> 00:20:02.037
Orphanages across the Western

00:20:02.037 --> 00:20:04.767
world were quietly closed down,

00:20:04.827 --> 00:20:07.327
or radically reformed, in favour

00:20:07.497 --> 00:20:09.807
of foster care and adoption.

00:20:10.847 --> 00:20:12.907
Hospital procedures changed.

00:20:13.377 --> 00:20:15.657
Mothers were no longer routinely

00:20:15.667 --> 00:20:17.687
separated from their newborns.

00:20:18.137 --> 00:20:21.157
Fathers were allowed into delivery rooms.

00:20:21.717 --> 00:20:23.847
Affection was encouraged.

00:20:24.717 --> 00:20:27.447
Essentially this transformed into

00:20:27.447 --> 00:20:29.887
the general guidance there is in most

00:20:29.897 --> 00:20:33.207
countries today, that infants need comfort

00:20:33.237 --> 00:20:35.667
and affection, that you should respond

00:20:35.707 --> 00:20:38.637
to your baby when it cries, and that

00:20:38.637 --> 00:20:41.167
holding and cuddling your child is not

00:20:41.167 --> 00:20:43.787
just nice for you and them, but necessary.

00:20:44.887 --> 00:20:47.257
It's perhaps easy for us today to

00:20:47.257 --> 00:20:49.137
say "of course this is true, of

00:20:49.137 --> 00:20:50.707
course babies need to be cuddled",

00:20:51.147 --> 00:20:54.747
but this was strictly advised against

00:20:55.007 --> 00:20:57.027
in the best-selling child-rearing

00:20:57.027 --> 00:20:59.397
manual of the early 20th century.

00:21:01.087 --> 00:21:05.147
And then, in 1989, came a tragic piece

00:21:05.377 --> 00:21:08.337
of human evidence that seemed to support

00:21:08.377 --> 00:21:10.667
the central lesson of Harlow’s work.

00:21:11.877 --> 00:21:14.327
In Romania, during the Ceaușescu

00:21:14.357 --> 00:21:17.087
dictatorship, huge numbers of children

00:21:17.337 --> 00:21:19.907
had passed through state orphanages.

00:21:20.897 --> 00:21:23.587
When he was overthrown and Western

00:21:23.617 --> 00:21:25.877
observers entered the country, they

00:21:25.877 --> 00:21:28.717
saw the scale of deprivation in these

00:21:28.737 --> 00:21:30.817
institutions for the first time.

00:21:31.797 --> 00:21:34.647
Tens of thousands of children had

00:21:34.647 --> 00:21:37.577
been fed and changed, but rarely held,

00:21:37.587 --> 00:21:40.697
rarely talked to, rarely played with.

00:21:41.757 --> 00:21:44.807
Many struggled for years afterwards, even

00:21:44.837 --> 00:21:47.587
after being adopted into loving families.

00:21:48.227 --> 00:21:50.577
Food, warmth and a bed

00:21:51.167 --> 00:21:52.237
had not been enough.

00:21:52.997 --> 00:21:54.777
Without comfort, attention and

00:21:54.777 --> 00:21:57.997
affection, many had been deeply scarred.

00:21:59.237 --> 00:22:02.107
Romania had become one of the most tragic

00:22:02.187 --> 00:22:04.937
natural experiments in early deprivation.

00:22:05.677 --> 00:22:07.841
It did not mirror Harlow’s

00:22:07.841 --> 00:22:09.907
laboratory work exactly, of course.

00:22:10.097 --> 00:22:13.137
But it powerfully supported his central

00:22:13.137 --> 00:22:16.727
idea: young primates, human or otherwise,

00:22:16.987 --> 00:22:19.427
need more than food to develop.

00:22:20.537 --> 00:22:22.867
Now, I haven't yet told you anything,

00:22:22.867 --> 00:22:25.127
really, about Harlow himself.

00:22:25.837 --> 00:22:27.937
He was, by most accounts,

00:22:28.187 --> 00:22:29.797
not a kind person.

00:22:30.517 --> 00:22:32.987
He had grown up in a household with a

00:22:33.027 --> 00:22:35.487
depressive father and a distant mother.

00:22:36.037 --> 00:22:38.437
He was sharp-tongued and often

00:22:38.487 --> 00:22:40.707
cruel to those who worked for him.

00:22:41.247 --> 00:22:43.287
He was married three times.

00:22:43.527 --> 00:22:44.747
He drank heavily.

00:22:45.137 --> 00:22:47.787
He suffered serious depression himself,

00:22:48.067 --> 00:22:50.927
severe enough that he was hospitalised and

00:22:50.937 --> 00:22:53.427
treated with electroconvulsive therapy.

00:22:54.467 --> 00:22:57.197
He seemed to show no affection towards

00:22:57.197 --> 00:23:00.067
his monkeys, once saying 'The only thing

00:23:00.067 --> 00:23:02.027
I care about is whether the monkeys

00:23:02.027 --> 00:23:03.787
will turn out a property I can publish.

00:23:04.157 --> 00:23:05.437
I don't have any love for them.

00:23:05.817 --> 00:23:06.467
Never have.

00:23:06.837 --> 00:23:08.077
I don't really like animals.

00:23:08.287 --> 00:23:10.437
I despise cats, I hate dogs.

00:23:10.717 --> 00:23:11.937
How could you like a monkey?'

00:23:12.877 --> 00:23:15.317
By his own description, he was a man

00:23:15.617 --> 00:23:18.097
who couldn't really love anything.

00:23:19.247 --> 00:23:20.394
It is, perhaps, odd.

00:23:21.031 --> 00:23:22.987
Here was a man whose greatest

00:23:23.007 --> 00:23:25.607
contribution to science was demonstrating

00:23:25.777 --> 00:23:27.557
that love and physical comfort

00:23:27.747 --> 00:23:30.237
were basic needs of all primates.

00:23:30.727 --> 00:23:32.967
Yet, by most accounts, he was

00:23:33.027 --> 00:23:34.937
incapable of giving either.

00:23:36.217 --> 00:23:38.547
Now, to wrap things up, there's one

00:23:38.547 --> 00:23:40.117
more thing worth saying about this

00:23:40.117 --> 00:23:42.567
experiment, and about Harlow's legacy.

00:23:43.237 --> 00:23:46.517
And that is that he made love measurable.

00:23:47.347 --> 00:23:49.697
Before Harlow, love had been

00:23:49.697 --> 00:23:51.707
the province of the poets.

00:23:51.887 --> 00:23:53.597
A thing of feeling, of

00:23:53.617 --> 00:23:55.687
metaphor, of sentiment.

00:23:56.777 --> 00:23:59.057
After Harlow, you could record

00:23:59.057 --> 00:24:01.407
it in an experimental log.

00:24:01.687 --> 00:24:03.817
You could draw a graph of it.

00:24:03.967 --> 00:24:06.327
You could measure, with cold

00:24:06.547 --> 00:24:09.047
scientific precision, the difference

00:24:09.297 --> 00:24:11.107
between a creature that had

00:24:11.137 --> 00:24:13.597
received it and one that had not.

00:24:14.357 --> 00:24:16.497
There is, maybe, something

00:24:16.517 --> 00:24:18.317
almost poetic about that.

00:24:18.807 --> 00:24:21.307
The cold-hearted, lonely man in

00:24:21.307 --> 00:24:23.587
his Wisconsin laboratory took the

00:24:23.587 --> 00:24:25.757
most romantic concept human beings

00:24:25.767 --> 00:24:28.487
have, and proved it was real.

00:24:30.817 --> 00:24:33.397
OK, then, that is it for today's episode,

00:24:33.587 --> 00:24:35.387
the second part of our three-part

00:24:35.387 --> 00:24:37.667
series on The Dark Side of Psychology.

00:24:38.067 --> 00:24:40.217
In our final part, we'll be heading

00:24:40.217 --> 00:24:43.007
to the psychiatric hospitals of 1970s

00:24:43.007 --> 00:24:45.467
America for the Rosenhan experiment,

00:24:45.667 --> 00:24:47.777
in which eight perfectly sane

00:24:47.777 --> 00:24:50.197
volunteers checked themselves in, and

00:24:50.197 --> 00:24:52.197
then had a much harder time getting

00:24:52.197 --> 00:24:53.867
out than they had bargained for.

00:24:54.617 --> 00:24:56.747
You've been listening to English Learning

00:24:56.747 --> 00:24:59.437
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:24:59.857 --> 00:25:02.827
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:25:02.867 --> 00:25:05.807
I'll catch you in the next episode.

